window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-06-3", "source": "2023年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the student union of your university is organizing an online discussion on college students doing community service. You are to write an essay on its benefits and the service you can provide to the community. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "These days, more and more college students are getting involved in community service. In my view, this is a\nmeaningful practice that benefits both the community and ourselves.\nCommunity service brings benefits to both society and the people who offer it. On the one hand, it warms our\nneighborhood: by visiting lonely elderly people or helping local children with their studies, we deliver real care\nto those in need. On the other hand, it shapes us as individuals, since serving others trains our communication\nskills and sense of responsibility. As for me, the service I can provide is teaching English. Being good at this\nsubject, I plan to offer free English lessons to children in my community every weekend, so that they can enjoy\nlearning and grow with confidence.\nIn short, doing community service is well worth our effort, as it helps others and improves ourselves at the\nsame time. I sincerely hope that more of us will step out and act."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国政府一直大力推行义务教育(compulsory education) ，以使每个儿童都享有受教育的机会。自1986年《义务教育法》生效以来，经过不懈努力，实现了在全国推行义务教育的目标。如今，在中国， 儿童年满六周岁开始上小学， 从小学到初中一共接受九年义务教育。从2008年秋季学期开始， 义务教育阶段学生无须缴纳学费。随着一系列教育改革举措的实施， 中国义务教育的质量也有显著提高。", "sample": "The Chinese government has been vigorously promoting compulsory education so that every child can have access to education. Since the Compulsory Education Law came into effect in 1986, China has, through unremitting efforts, achieved the goal of making compulsory education available nationwide. Today in China, children start primary school at the age of six and receive a total of nine years of compulsory education from primary school to junior high school. Since the autumn semester of 2008, students in the compulsory education stage have no longer had to pay tuition fees. With the implementation of a series of educational reform measures, the quality of compulsory education in China has also improved significantly."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Whether you're just having a down day or a down period, taking a walk can instantly lift your mood, especially when you go outdoors. Not only can walking make you less depressed, but according to a new study, depression sufferers who took a  __26__  walk showed just as much improvement in their  __27__  as people taking medicine. In fact, 60-70 percent of the participants in the study could no longer even be  __28__  as depressed.\nBone density may not be one of the most exciting health benefits of walking, but it's an important one. People with stronger bones avoid osteoporosis(骨质疏松症) and all the problems that  __29__  with it, like breaks and other disabilities. And the best way to get strong, healthy bones is by doing weight-bearing exercises like running, dancing and walking, according to a large study. But as regards bones, it's  __30__  use it or lose it: To keep your bones strong you have to keep exercising. The researchers found that adults who walked regularly had better bone density throughout their lives than their  __31__  friends.\nTaking a walk can be great for  __32__  your head or blowing off some steam. It also provides a great opportunity to  __33__  with friends and family, far away from electronics and other  __34__  at home. Even better, you set a powerful example because when they see you  __35__  the benefits of walking, they'll be encouraged to walk more, too.", "matchIntro": "A Club Where Lions Dance and Traditions Take Root", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The first thing you notice in the staircase to the fourth-floor studio on Canal Street in Manhattan, New York, is the measured thumping(嘭嘭声) coming from behind a metal door. Just beyond the entrance, large lion masks were twisting and turning to the drum-beat. On a recent Friday evening, the teenagers made their way across the studio floor as they practiced Chinese lion dancing."}, {"label": "B", "text": "\"You want to play in a circle,\" Victor Fong, aged 24, told his students at the New York Chinese Freemasons Athletic Club. \"Take it slow and do it again.\" The dance group, made up of 60 members, performs throughout the year but was now preparing for its biggest events, Chinese Lunar New Year celebrations, which will begin on Saturday and conclude on February 15. Teenagers comprise about half of the group, many of whom began lion dancing at the age of 14. About 100 current and past members of the dance group—which has been performing since 1956—will be among the 220 groups marching through Chinatown in Manhattan for the 18th Lunar New Year Parade on February 5."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mr. Fong has been involved in the organization since he was 15 and began teaching lion dancing three years ago. But the club also acts as a recreation center and safe haven(庇护所) for teenagers, with video games readily available. As many as 20 students show up after school. \"The basic requirement for hanging out here is you have to learn how to lion dance,\" Mr. Fong said. While that is not a formal requirement for being a member, everyone finds a way to participate in the Lunar New Year Parade, whether it's by dancing, carrying a flag or beating a drum."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Alvin Chau, aged 26, is an environmental consultant by day and a lion dancer on weekends. He has been a club member for 10 years and joined because of an interest in lion dance. \"We're a big family,\" he said, shaking hands with other members as they walked through the door. \"You know everyone.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "It is believed that the lion dance began in the third century. Stories vary about how lion dancing came to be, but most of them include a monster named Nian who would terrorize a village. The villagers finally banded together and scared the beast away with firecrackers(爆竹) and drums. While lions are not native to China, some versions of the story include the villagers creating a monster of their own in the shape of a lion to fight off the beast."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Today, the dancers travel across Chinatown going from business to business to bring good luck for the coming year. The new year—according to the Chinese lunar calendar—will begin on Saturday and marks the Year of the Rooster(鸡年), which is thought to be a symbol of positivity. \"It's almost like the dark night is ending and the sun is coming up,\" said Ya Yung Teng, the digital collections coordinator for the Museum of Chinese in America. \"It's hopeful that we're going to have a new day.\" Roosters and chickens are not particularly strong creatures, Ms. Teng said, but they are numerous. \"In a way,\" she said, the rooster \"stands for 'We the People.'\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "The lion head and tail are operated by two people. As one person controls the head, a second follows under a train of fabric representing the body. The dancers move in harmony as they imitate the animal's natural movements in the wild, including the aggressiveness of an attack. A lion head figure weighs under 10 pounds and sits squarely on the dancer's shoulders. Inside, the dancer manipulates strings that open and close the lion's eyes, shake its ears and open its mouth to reveal a fire-orange tongue."}, {"label": "H", "text": "\"A good lion dancer will imitate a living creature,\" said Karlin Chan, aged 59, who heads the athletic club's community public relations. \"I started lion dancing when I was a kid,\" he said. \"Chinatown was much smaller then and it was a celebration with fireworks and firecrackers, which added a lot of flavor and meaning to it.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "Mr. Chan buys a new lion head each year when he travels to China. A head costs about $1,500. \"If you want the good stuff, the quality, you have to see it for yourself,\" he said. \"I'll inspect the product before we put it in the container and ship it over.\" Mr. Chan, who has been involved with the club for nearly 50 years, said that passing the dance from one generation to the next was vital. \"You need to pass on the traditions and the culture, and this is a part of our culture,\" he said. \"It's a great way to promote cultural understanding and exchange; we welcome that.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "For Sara Pore, aged 17, another club member, lion dance is more than just tradition; it provides a creative outlet. \"Lion dancing started 2,000 years ago—that's incredible,\" she said. \"But what makes you a competent lion dancer is that there's a sense of imagination involved. Lion dancing teaches competence in leadership because of this. You're constantly forced to push yourself past your limit.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Back at rehearsal(排练), Just in Le, aged 18, tied a red ribbon around his waist to practice jumping. The dancers use the ribbons to pull themselves up over their partners' heads. The room's ceilings are too low to wear the lion heads for jumping practice, so once up on his partner's head, Mr. Le held out his arms as if in offering."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Mr. Le comes by the dance as a legacy(传承). \"I was born into it,\" Mr. Le said, noting that his uncle and father were club members. \"Growing up, I would always watch my family and see the lion dance, and I slowly grew interested in it.\" By the age of 14, he was fully enrolled in the athletic club's lion dance group. \"I value my culture and tradition, being Chinese or Asian-American. I have a lot of pride in that,\" he said. \"I want to contribute and give back to the community.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Team-building exercises have become popular for managers trying to increase organisational and team harmony and productivity. Unfortunately many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance.\nA paper published this week by University of Sydney researchers in Social Networks has reported participants' feelings about team-building interventions(干预), revealing ethical implications in forcing employees to take part.\n\"Many people see team-building activities as a waste of time, so we decided to look in more depth at what's behind this,\" said the paper's lead researcher, Dr. Peter Matous.\n\"Teams are formed, combined and restructured. Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned. All this is done with the aim of improving workplace efficiency, collaboration and cohesion. But does any of this work?\" said Dr. Matous.\nThe study found that team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening into personal attitudes and relationships between team members were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive, although the researchers say some degree of openness and vulnerability is often necessary to make deep, effective connections with colleagues.\n\"Some participants were against team-building exercises because they were implicitly compulsory. They didn't welcome management's interest in their lives beyond their direct work performance,\" said Matous. \"Many people don't want to be forced into having fun or making friends, especially not on top of their busy jobs. They feel management is being too nosy or trying to control their lives too much.\"\nIn this study the researchers recommended a self-disclosure(表露) approach where participants were guided through a series of questions that allowed them to increasingly disclose personal information and values. The method is well-tested and has been shown to increase interpersonal closeness. However, to be successful it must be voluntary.\nThe researchers said there are numerous schools of thought that propose differing psychological methods for strengthening relationships. \"With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed from other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively to strengthen an entire collective, which is more than just the sum of individual relationships,\" said Dr. Matous.", "47": "Team-building exercises have become popular for managers trying to increase organisational and team harmony and productivity. Unfortunately many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance.\nA paper published this week by University of Sydney researchers in Social Networks has reported participants' feelings about team-building interventions(干预), revealing ethical implications in forcing employees to take part.\n\"Many people see team-building activities as a waste of time, so we decided to look in more depth at what's behind this,\" said the paper's lead researcher, Dr. Peter Matous.\n\"Teams are formed, combined and restructured. Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned. All this is done with the aim of improving workplace efficiency, collaboration and cohesion. But does any of this work?\" said Dr. Matous.\nThe study found that team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening into personal attitudes and relationships between team members were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive, although the researchers say some degree of openness and vulnerability is often necessary to make deep, effective connections with colleagues.\n\"Some participants were against team-building exercises because they were implicitly compulsory. They didn't welcome management's interest in their lives beyond their direct work performance,\" said Matous. \"Many people don't want to be forced into having fun or making friends, especially not on top of their busy jobs. They feel management is being too nosy or trying to control their lives too much.\"\nIn this study the researchers recommended a self-disclosure(表露) approach where participants were guided through a series of questions that allowed them to increasingly disclose personal information and values. The method is well-tested and has been shown to increase interpersonal closeness. However, to be successful it must be voluntary.\nThe researchers said there are numerous schools of thought that propose differing psychological methods for strengthening relationships. \"With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed from other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively to strengthen an entire collective, which is more than just the sum of individual relationships,\" said Dr. Matous.", "48": "Team-building exercises have become popular for managers trying to increase organisational and team harmony and productivity. Unfortunately many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance.\nA paper published this week by University of Sydney researchers in Social Networks has reported participants' feelings about team-building interventions(干预), revealing ethical implications in forcing employees to take part.\n\"Many people see team-building activities as a waste of time, so we decided to look in more depth at what's behind this,\" said the paper's lead researcher, Dr. Peter Matous.\n\"Teams are formed, combined and restructured. Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned. All this is done with the aim of improving workplace efficiency, collaboration and cohesion. But does any of this work?\" said Dr. Matous.\nThe study found that team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening into personal attitudes and relationships between team members were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive, although the researchers say some degree of openness and vulnerability is often necessary to make deep, effective connections with colleagues.\n\"Some participants were against team-building exercises because they were implicitly compulsory. They didn't welcome management's interest in their lives beyond their direct work performance,\" said Matous. \"Many people don't want to be forced into having fun or making friends, especially not on top of their busy jobs. They feel management is being too nosy or trying to control their lives too much.\"\nIn this study the researchers recommended a self-disclosure(表露) approach where participants were guided through a series of questions that allowed them to increasingly disclose personal information and values. The method is well-tested and has been shown to increase interpersonal closeness. However, to be successful it must be voluntary.\nThe researchers said there are numerous schools of thought that propose differing psychological methods for strengthening relationships. \"With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed from other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively to strengthen an entire collective, which is more than just the sum of individual relationships,\" said Dr. Matous.", "49": "Team-building exercises have become popular for managers trying to increase organisational and team harmony and productivity. Unfortunately many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance.\nA paper published this week by University of Sydney researchers in Social Networks has reported participants' feelings about team-building interventions(干预), revealing ethical implications in forcing employees to take part.\n\"Many people see team-building activities as a waste of time, so we decided to look in more depth at what's behind this,\" said the paper's lead researcher, Dr. Peter Matous.\n\"Teams are formed, combined and restructured. Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned. All this is done with the aim of improving workplace efficiency, collaboration and cohesion. But does any of this work?\" said Dr. Matous.\nThe study found that team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening into personal attitudes and relationships between team members were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive, although the researchers say some degree of openness and vulnerability is often necessary to make deep, effective connections with colleagues.\n\"Some participants were against team-building exercises because they were implicitly compulsory. They didn't welcome management's interest in their lives beyond their direct work performance,\" said Matous. \"Many people don't want to be forced into having fun or making friends, especially not on top of their busy jobs. They feel management is being too nosy or trying to control their lives too much.\"\nIn this study the researchers recommended a self-disclosure(表露) approach where participants were guided through a series of questions that allowed them to increasingly disclose personal information and values. The method is well-tested and has been shown to increase interpersonal closeness. However, to be successful it must be voluntary.\nThe researchers said there are numerous schools of thought that propose differing psychological methods for strengthening relationships. \"With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed from other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively to strengthen an entire collective, which is more than just the sum of individual relationships,\" said Dr. Matous.", "50": "Team-building exercises have become popular for managers trying to increase organisational and team harmony and productivity. Unfortunately many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance.\nA paper published this week by University of Sydney researchers in Social Networks has reported participants' feelings about team-building interventions(干预), revealing ethical implications in forcing employees to take part.\n\"Many people see team-building activities as a waste of time, so we decided to look in more depth at what's behind this,\" said the paper's lead researcher, Dr. Peter Matous.\n\"Teams are formed, combined and restructured. Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned. All this is done with the aim of improving workplace efficiency, collaboration and cohesion. But does any of this work?\" said Dr. Matous.\nThe study found that team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening into personal attitudes and relationships between team members were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive, although the researchers say some degree of openness and vulnerability is often necessary to make deep, effective connections with colleagues.\n\"Some participants were against team-building exercises because they were implicitly compulsory. They didn't welcome management's interest in their lives beyond their direct work performance,\" said Matous. \"Many people don't want to be forced into having fun or making friends, especially not on top of their busy jobs. They feel management is being too nosy or trying to control their lives too much.\"\nIn this study the researchers recommended a self-disclosure(表露) approach where participants were guided through a series of questions that allowed them to increasingly disclose personal information and values. The method is well-tested and has been shown to increase interpersonal closeness. However, to be successful it must be voluntary.\nThe researchers said there are numerous schools of thought that propose differing psychological methods for strengthening relationships. \"With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed from other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively to strengthen an entire collective, which is more than just the sum of individual relationships,\" said Dr. Matous.", "51": "There are close to 58,000 homeless people in Los Angeles county. That's a shocking and tragic number for a region that's home to some of the richest people in the world. At last, the problem became so acute—and so visible—that Los Angeles took extraordinary action. The citizens of this county voted in November 2016 and again in March 2017 to raise their own taxes. The purpose of such action is to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of housing and social services for the homeless.\nAs a result, Los Angeles now has its best chance in decades to combat homelessness. This is an opportunity that surely all can agree must not be wasted. It is neither desirable nor morally acceptable nor practical for this county to tolerate the signs of deprivation more commonly associated with the slums(贫民窟) of Rio de Janiero.\nHow did we get here? For as long as there are homeless people, there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness, of laziness. But contrary to popular belief, the homeless in Los Angeles are not mostly mentally ill or drug addicted or frightening, although a sizable minority meet some of those descriptions. Today, a greater and greater proportion of people living on the streets are there because of bad luck or a series of mistakes, or because the economy forgot them—they lost a job or were evicted(逐出) or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving.\nThe challenges are enormous, even if everyone is pulling in the same direction. That reality was driven home this month by a new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report showing that the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing.\nIn the world's richest nation, homelessness on this scale should be shameful and shocking. But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed. Increasingly, we are uncomfortable, annoyed, disgusted, scared or unaware. Compassion(同情心) is being replaced by resignation.", "52": "There are close to 58,000 homeless people in Los Angeles county. That's a shocking and tragic number for a region that's home to some of the richest people in the world. At last, the problem became so acute—and so visible—that Los Angeles took extraordinary action. The citizens of this county voted in November 2016 and again in March 2017 to raise their own taxes. The purpose of such action is to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of housing and social services for the homeless.\nAs a result, Los Angeles now has its best chance in decades to combat homelessness. This is an opportunity that surely all can agree must not be wasted. It is neither desirable nor morally acceptable nor practical for this county to tolerate the signs of deprivation more commonly associated with the slums(贫民窟) of Rio de Janiero.\nHow did we get here? For as long as there are homeless people, there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness, of laziness. But contrary to popular belief, the homeless in Los Angeles are not mostly mentally ill or drug addicted or frightening, although a sizable minority meet some of those descriptions. Today, a greater and greater proportion of people living on the streets are there because of bad luck or a series of mistakes, or because the economy forgot them—they lost a job or were evicted(逐出) or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving.\nThe challenges are enormous, even if everyone is pulling in the same direction. That reality was driven home this month by a new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report showing that the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing.\nIn the world's richest nation, homelessness on this scale should be shameful and shocking. But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed. Increasingly, we are uncomfortable, annoyed, disgusted, scared or unaware. Compassion(同情心) is being replaced by resignation.", "53": "There are close to 58,000 homeless people in Los Angeles county. That's a shocking and tragic number for a region that's home to some of the richest people in the world. At last, the problem became so acute—and so visible—that Los Angeles took extraordinary action. The citizens of this county voted in November 2016 and again in March 2017 to raise their own taxes. The purpose of such action is to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of housing and social services for the homeless.\nAs a result, Los Angeles now has its best chance in decades to combat homelessness. This is an opportunity that surely all can agree must not be wasted. It is neither desirable nor morally acceptable nor practical for this county to tolerate the signs of deprivation more commonly associated with the slums(贫民窟) of Rio de Janiero.\nHow did we get here? For as long as there are homeless people, there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness, of laziness. But contrary to popular belief, the homeless in Los Angeles are not mostly mentally ill or drug addicted or frightening, although a sizable minority meet some of those descriptions. Today, a greater and greater proportion of people living on the streets are there because of bad luck or a series of mistakes, or because the economy forgot them—they lost a job or were evicted(逐出) or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving.\nThe challenges are enormous, even if everyone is pulling in the same direction. That reality was driven home this month by a new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report showing that the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing.\nIn the world's richest nation, homelessness on this scale should be shameful and shocking. But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed. Increasingly, we are uncomfortable, annoyed, disgusted, scared or unaware. Compassion(同情心) is being replaced by resignation.", "54": "There are close to 58,000 homeless people in Los Angeles county. That's a shocking and tragic number for a region that's home to some of the richest people in the world. At last, the problem became so acute—and so visible—that Los Angeles took extraordinary action. The citizens of this county voted in November 2016 and again in March 2017 to raise their own taxes. The purpose of such action is to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of housing and social services for the homeless.\nAs a result, Los Angeles now has its best chance in decades to combat homelessness. This is an opportunity that surely all can agree must not be wasted. It is neither desirable nor morally acceptable nor practical for this county to tolerate the signs of deprivation more commonly associated with the slums(贫民窟) of Rio de Janiero.\nHow did we get here? For as long as there are homeless people, there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness, of laziness. But contrary to popular belief, the homeless in Los Angeles are not mostly mentally ill or drug addicted or frightening, although a sizable minority meet some of those descriptions. Today, a greater and greater proportion of people living on the streets are there because of bad luck or a series of mistakes, or because the economy forgot them—they lost a job or were evicted(逐出) or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving.\nThe challenges are enormous, even if everyone is pulling in the same direction. That reality was driven home this month by a new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report showing that the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing.\nIn the world's richest nation, homelessness on this scale should be shameful and shocking. But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed. Increasingly, we are uncomfortable, annoyed, disgusted, scared or unaware. Compassion(同情心) is being replaced by resignation.", "55": "There are close to 58,000 homeless people in Los Angeles county. That's a shocking and tragic number for a region that's home to some of the richest people in the world. At last, the problem became so acute—and so visible—that Los Angeles took extraordinary action. The citizens of this county voted in November 2016 and again in March 2017 to raise their own taxes. The purpose of such action is to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of housing and social services for the homeless.\nAs a result, Los Angeles now has its best chance in decades to combat homelessness. This is an opportunity that surely all can agree must not be wasted. It is neither desirable nor morally acceptable nor practical for this county to tolerate the signs of deprivation more commonly associated with the slums(贫民窟) of Rio de Janiero.\nHow did we get here? For as long as there are homeless people, there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness, of laziness. But contrary to popular belief, the homeless in Los Angeles are not mostly mentally ill or drug addicted or frightening, although a sizable minority meet some of those descriptions. Today, a greater and greater proportion of people living on the streets are there because of bad luck or a series of mistakes, or because the economy forgot them—they lost a job or were evicted(逐出) or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving.\nThe challenges are enormous, even if everyone is pulling in the same direction. That reality was driven home this month by a new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report showing that the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing.\nIn the world's richest nation, homelessness on this scale should be shameful and shocking. But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed. Increasingly, we are uncomfortable, annoyed, disgusted, scared or unaware. Compassion(同情心) is being replaced by resignation."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) daily\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 walk 之间作定语\n【依据】\"每日散步\"才有持续疗效\n本句把走路效果与 people taking medicine\"吃药\"相比——吃药是规律按日进行的，与之\n相提并论的走路自然是\"每日的\"daily walk，强调坚持，才能解释 60-70 percent of the\nparticipants... could no longer even be __(28)__ as depressed 的明显改善。\n【竞争词】O) typical 义不贴语境\ntypical walk 是\"典型的一次散步\"，无法体现\"像吃药一样规律坚持\"的疗效逻辑，与下文显著改\n善对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) symptoms\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词性物主代词 their 后必跟名词\n【依据】抑郁\"症状\"的改善才对应吃药\n本句说抑郁症患者走路后 showed just as much improvement in their __(27)__ as\npeople taking medicine——和\"吃药的人\"作对比，能像吃药一样改善的、抑郁者身上的东\n西，正是\"症状\"。symptoms（症状）扣住了上文 depression sufferers 的语境。\n【竞争词】I) distractions 语义不搭\ndistractions 是\"分心物\"，与\"抑郁改善\"无关，且它是第 34 空的语境用词。\nL) occurrences 义偏且无所属\noccurrences 指\"发生的事件\"，说\"他们的事件改善了\"逻辑不通。\nC) bond 名义不合\nbond 作名词是\"纽带\"，与抑郁症状无关，它另有动词用法留给第 33 空。\nE) clearing 动名词义不对\nclearing 作名词是\"清理\"这一动作，放\"their clearing\"既不通顺也不切题。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) classified\n动词\n【词性槽】be 之后、过去分词构成被动\n【依据】be classified as 固定搭配\"被归类为\"\nbe classified as 是固定搭配，意为\"被归类/划分为\"。could no longer even be __(28)__\nas depressed\"不再能被归类为抑郁\"正合上文\"症状像吃药一样改善\"，逻辑顺畅。\n【竞争词】B) approved 搭配与义皆不合\napproved as 不是\"被认定为\"的常用搭配，且 approved（批准、认可）说\"被认可为抑郁\"语义\n荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) come\n动词\n【词性槽】定语从句 that 后缺谓语\n【依据】come with\"伴随……而来\"\ncome with 意为\"随……一起出现、伴随而来\"。all the problems that __(29)__ with it, like\nbreaks and other disabilities\"伴随骨质疏松而来的所有问题，如骨折等\"，come with 既\n搭配成立、复数 problems 对应原形 come，语法语义双通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) definitely\n副词\n【词性槽】be 与短语之间作状语强调\n【依据】强调\"确实是用进废退\"\n本句承上文\"想骨骼强壮就得一直锻炼\"，it's __(30)__ use it or lose it\"这确实是用进废退\"，\ndefinitely（确实地）加强\"就是这么个道理\"的肯定语气，与 To keep your bones strong\nyou have to keep exercising 的告诫一致。\n【竞争词】J) exclusively 义不合\nexclusively 是\"唯一地、专门地\"，\"这唯一地是用进废退\"语义别扭，无法表达对该道理的确认\n强调。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— exclusively 词性对而语气错。exclusively 与 definitely 同\n为副词，词性这关骗得过；防坑靠掂句子语气——it's __(30)__ use it or lose it 要的是\"确\n实如此\"的确认，不是\"唯一\"的限定，副词比选务必落到语气逻辑上。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) inactive\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 friends 前作定语\n【依据】与\"经常走路\"形成对比\n本句说 adults who walked regularly had better bone density... than their __(31)__\nfriends——\"经常走路\"的人骨密度更好，对比的对象自然是\"不爱运动的\"朋友。inactive\n（不活动的、久坐不动的）正好与 walked regularly 反向呼应，对比成立。\n【竞争词】O) typical 不构成对比\ntypical 是\"典型的\"，\"典型的朋友\"既无对比意味也不切骨骼话题；本句要的是与\"常运动\"相反的\n一群人，typical 给不出这层反差。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) clearing\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 for 之后接动名词\n【依据】clear your head\"理清思绪\"\nclear one's head 意为\"清醒头脑、理清思绪\"。great for __(32)__ your head or blowing\noff some steam\"利于清空头脑或发泄压力\"，clearing 与并列的 blowing off steam（释放\n压力）同属\"放松减压\"语义，贴合本段走路怡心的主旨。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) bond\n动词\n【词性槽】to 不定式后接动词原形\n【依据】bond with sb.\"与某人增进感情\"\nbond with 意为\"与……建立情感纽带、增进感情\"。a great opportunity to __(33)__ with\nfriends and family\"与亲友增进感情的好机会\"，正合本段走路有益人际关系的主旨。\n【竞争词】A) accompany 及物动词不接 with\naccompany 是及物动词，\"陪伴某人\"直接说 accompany sb.，不说 accompany with sb.；空\n后有 with，accompany 在语法搭配上就不成立。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— accompany 义近却搭配错。accompany\"陪伴\"词义看着就\n是\"与亲友相处\"，极易误选；防坑靠盯空后介词——accompany 及物不接 with，而 to\n__(33)__ with friends 后面有 with，只有 bond with 成立。先看搭配、别只看词义。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) distractions\n名词\n【词性槽】other 后接名词、与 electronics 并列\n【依据】电子产品等\"分心物\"\n本句说走路让人 far away from electronics and other __(34)__ at home\"远离电子产品和\n家里其他的__\"，与 electronics 并列、又能让人\"远离\"的，正是\"分心的事物\"distractions\n——电子产品本身就是典型的 distraction，并列贴切。\n【竞争词】L) occurrences 义偏、与并列项不同类\noccurrences 是\"发生的事件\"，与 electronics（电子产品这类\"物\"）不是同类并列项，且\"远离\n家里其他事件\"逻辑生硬。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompany", "B) approved", "C) bond", "D) classified", "E) clearing", "F) come", "G) daily", "H) definitely", "I) distractions", "J) exclusively", "K) inactive", "L) occurrences", "M) reaping", "N) symptoms", "O) typical"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) reaping\n动词\n【词性槽】see sb. do/doing 中作宾补的动词\n【依据】reap the benefits 固定搭配\"收获好处\"\nreap the benefits 是固定搭配，意为\"获得/收获好处\"。when they see you __(35)__ the\nbenefits of walking\"看见你收获走路的好处\"，正承接后半句 they'll be encouraged to\nwalk more\"于是受激励也多走\"，逻辑顺。\n【竞争词】A) accompany 搭配不成立\naccompany 是\"陪伴\"，accompany the benefits（陪伴好处）讲不通，且与\"被激励多走\"的因\n果链断裂。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "The lion dancers perform from door to door to bring New Year wishes to business people.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】挨家走访商铺送新年祝福\n【定位】Today, the dancers travel across Chinatown going from business to business to bring good\nluck for the coming year.\n如今，舞者们穿行于唐人街，挨家挨户走访商铺，为即将到来的一年带来好运。\n【改写】perform from door to door ↔ going from business to business\n挨家挨户=挨个商铺\nbring New Year wishes to business people ↔ bring good luck for the coming year\n新年祝福=带来来年好运", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The New York dance club also serves as a place for entertainment where youngsters can enjoy themselves safely.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】俱乐部兼作安全娱乐场所\n【定位】But the club also acts as a recreation center and safe haven for teenagers, with video games\nreadily available.\n但这家俱乐部也充当青少年的娱乐中心和安全庇护所，备有随时可玩的电子游戏。\n【改写】a place for entertainment ↔ a recreation center\n娱乐场所=娱乐中心\nyoungsters can enjoy themselves safely ↔ safe haven for teenagers\n安全玩乐=安全庇护所", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Lion dancers need to have a little imagination to perform well.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】需要一点想象力\n【定位】But what makes you a competent lion dancer is that there's a sense of imagination involved.\n但让你成为一名称职舞狮者的，是其中需要有一种想象力。\n【改写】have a little imagination to perform well ↔\nwhat makes you a competent lion dancer is that there's a sense of imagination involved\n想象力造就称职舞者", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "There are a number of different versions about the origin of the lion dance.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】起源有多个不同版本\n【定位】Stories vary about how lion dancing came to be, but most of them include a monster named\nNian who would terrorize a village.\n关于舞狮如何起源，说法各异，但大多数版本都包含一个会恐吓村庄、名叫年的怪兽。\n【改写】a number of different versions ↔ Stories vary\n多个不同版本=故事各异\nthe origin of the lion dance ↔ how lion dancing came to be\n起源=如何产生", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Some 50% of the members of a New York lion dance club are teenagers.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】约 50% 成员是青少年\n【定位】Teenagers comprise about half of the group, many of whom began lion dancing at the age\nof 14.\n青少年约占该团体的一半，其中许多人在 14 岁时开始舞狮。\n【改写】Some 50% of the members ↔ about half of the group\n50%=约一半\nare teenagers ↔ Teenagers comprise\n主体都是青少年\n【辨邻】 C C 段 As many as 20 students show up after school 给的是放学后到场的 20 人，讲\n的是出勤人数，并未说青少年占成员的‘一半’，故不是 40 题源头。\nAs many as 20 students show up after school", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "One club member says he feels proud of his cultural tradition.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为自己的文化传统自豪\n【定位】I value my culture and tradition, being Chinese or Asian-American. I have a lot of pride\nin that.\n作为华裔或亚裔美国人，我珍视自己的文化与传统。我为此深感自豪。\n【改写】feels proud of his cultural tradition ↔ I value my culture and tradition\n珍视＝自豪的来源\nfeels proud ↔ I have a lot of pride in that\n感到自豪=心怀自豪\n【辨邻】 I I 段 You need to pass on the traditions and the culture 强调要把文化传下去，是\n‘传承’主题，没有 feels proud 这层自豪情感，故非 41 题源头。\nYou need to pass on the traditions and the culture, and this is a part of our culture", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Two dancers coordinate their movements, one manipulating the lion's head and the other its body.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】两人分别操控狮头与狮身\n【定位】The lion head and tail are operated by two people. As one person controls the head, a second\nfollows under a train of fabric representing the body.\n狮头和狮尾由两人操作。一人控制狮头，另一人在代表狮身的一长片布料下跟随。\n【改写】Two dancers coordinate their movements ↔ The lion head and tail are operated by two people\n两名舞者协作\none manipulating the lion's head and the other its body ↔\nAs one person controls the head, a second follows under a train of fabric representing the body\n一控头一控身", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "One lion dancer compares their New York dance club to a family.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】把俱乐部比作一个家庭\n【定位】\"We're a big family,\" he said, shaking hands with other members as they walked through\nthe door.\n“我们是一个大家庭，”他一边说，一边与走进门来的其他成员握手。\n【改写】compares their New York dance club to a family ↔ We're a big family\n说是家庭=比作家庭", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Lion dance should be handed down to future generations as part of Chinese culture.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】应作为文化代代相传\n【定位】Mr. Chan, who has been involved with the club for nearly 50 years, said that passing the\ndance from one generation to the next was vital.\n与俱乐部相伴近五十年的陈先生说，把这门舞蹈代代相传至关重要。\n【改写】handed down to future generations ↔ passing the dance from one generation to the next\n传给后代=代代相传\nas part of Chinese culture ↔ this is a part of our culture\n中华文化的一部分\n【辨邻】 L L 段 I want to contribute and give back to the community 是个人意愿，未提‘把舞\n狮传给未来世代至关重要’这一普遍主张，故非 44 题源头。\nI value my culture and tradition, being Chinese or Asian-American", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "One lion dancer learned how to perform from his elders.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】从长辈处学会舞狮\n【定位】\"Growing up, I would always watch my family and see the lion dance, and I slowly grew\ninterested in it.\"\n“成长过程中，我总是看着家人舞狮，慢慢地就对它产生了兴趣。”\n【改写】from his elders ↔ his uncle and father were club members\n长辈=叔叔和父亲\nlearned how to perform ↔\nI would always watch my family and see the lion dance, and I slowly grew interested in it\n看着家人学会\n【辨邻】 H H 段 I started lion dancing when I was a kid 只讲儿时起步，没有 from his elders\n这层家传习得，故非 45 题源头。\nI started lion dancing when I was a kid\nI I 段 passing the dance from one generation to the next 讲把舞狮传给后代，与 45\n题‘从长辈处学会’方向相反，故非源头。\npassing the dance from one generation to the next was vital", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why are many employees opposed to team-building exercises?", "options": ["A) They consider such exercises annoying.", "B) They deem these exercises counter-productive.", "C) They see such exercises as harmful to harmony.", "D) They find these exercises too demanding."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why are many employees opposed to team-building exercises? 用 Why 提问，问的是\n原文已写明的具体理由，不需要跨段推理。\n【拆句】题干 Why are many employees opposed to team-building exercises? 是一般疑问句，\n主干为员工反对团建练习，be opposed to 是固定搭配表示反对、抵触，结构平铺直叙，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Unfortunately many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises\nas a nuisance.\n遗憾的是，许多员工反感被强制团建，并常把这些练习视为一种麻烦。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nregard these exercises as a nuisance ↔ consider such exercises annoying\nresent ↔ annoying\n预判员工嫌团建烦人，They consider such exercises annoying 正是定位句 regard\nthese exercises as a nuisance 的同义改写，故锁定该项。\nB) 无中生有\ncounter-productive 指适得其反、降低生产力，原文只说员工嫌烦，从未说团建会起反\n作用，属凭空添加。\nC) 与原文相反\nharmful to harmony 称团建有害于和谐，但原文 increase organisational and team\nharmony 说团建本意是增进和谐，选项把目的反说成危害。\nD) 答非所问\ntoo demanding 指要求过高、太费力，这是 on top of their busy jobs 一带而过的另\n一层意思，并非第 46 题问的反对的主要原因。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did Dr. Matous and his team do to find out whether team-building activities would improve productivity?", "options": ["A) They relocated team leaders and their offices.", "B) They rearranged the staff and office spaces.", "C) They redesigned the staff's work schedules.", "D) They reintroduced some cohesive activities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What did Dr. Matous and his team do 问的是研究团队具体做了什么，答案是原文明写的\n动作清单，属事实查找。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What did Dr. Matous and his team do，后接不定式目的状语 to find out\nwhether...，其中 whether 引导宾语从句表示是否提升效率；理清主干问做法、状语说目\n的，即无障碍。\n【定位】Teams are formed, combined and restructured. Staff are relocated and office spaces\nredesigned. All this is done with the aim of improving workplace efficiency, collaboration\nand cohesion.\n团队被组建、合并、重组。员工被调岗，办公空间被重新设计。所有这些都是为了提升工作\n效率、协作与凝聚力。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nrelocated team leaders 只说调动了团队领导，而原文 Staff are relocated 指全体员\n工，选项把范围悄悄缩小到领导一类人。\nB) ✓ 正确\nStaff are relocated ↔ rearranged the staff\noffice spaces redesigned ↔ office spaces\n预判为调动员工与办公空间，They rearranged the staff and office spaces 把两个动\n作合并改写，与定位句一一对应，故锁定该项。\nC) 偷换概念\nredesigned the staff's work schedules 说重新设计的是工作日程，但原文\nredesigned 的对象是 office spaces 办公空间，选项偷换了被改造的对象。\nD) 无中生有\nreintroduced some cohesive activities 说重新引入凝聚性活动，原文从未提及重新引\n入活动这一动作，纯属添加。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What did the study by Matous' team find about some team-building exercises?", "options": ["A) They were intended to share personal attitudes and relationships.", "B) They implicitly added to the vulnerability of team members.", "C) They invariably strengthened connections among colleagues.", "D) They were regarded as an intrusion into employees' private lives."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did the study by Matous' team find about some team-building exercises? 问的\n是研究已写明的发现，可直接定位明文结论，属细节查找。\n【拆句】题干 What did the study by Matous' team find about some team-building exercises?\n主干为研究发现了什么，about 引出对象范围；注意 some 限定的是部分团建练习而非全\n部，是本题理解关键。\n【定位】The study found that team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening\ninto personal attitudes and relationships between team members were considered too heavy-\nhanded and intrusive, although the researchers say some degree of openness and vulnerability\nis often necessary to make deep, effective connections with colleagues.\n研究发现，那些聚焦于分享并干预团队成员个人态度与关系的团建练习，被认为过于强硬且\n具有侵入性，尽管研究者表示，某种程度的开放与脆弱往往是与同事建立深层有效联系所必\n需的。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nintended to share personal attitudes and relationships 说团建意在分享个人态度关\n系，但 focused on the sharing of... 是研究者对这类团建特征的客观描述，且本题问的\n是研究的发现而非团建的意图。\nB) 与原文相反\nadded to the vulnerability 称团建增加了成员的脆弱，但原文 some degree\nof...vulnerability is often necessary 说一定程度的脆弱是建立联系所必需的，并非团建\n恶意制造脆弱。\nC) 过度推断\ninvariably strengthened connections 说团建无一例外地增进了联系，但原文只说脆\n弱开放是建立联系所必需的前提，且整段强调这类团建被嫌侵入，并未说它总能增进\n联系。\nD) ✓ 正确\nintrusive ↔ an intrusion into employees' private lives\ninterest in their lives beyond their direct work performance ↔ into employees' private lives\n预判某团建被嫌强硬侵入，They were regarded as an intrusion into employees'\nprivate lives 把 intrusive 与 P6 对私生活的窥探合并改写，完全对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "How can the self-disclosure approach succeed in increasing interpersonal closeness?", "options": ["A) By allowing participants freedom to express themselves.", "B) By applying it to employees who volunteer to participate.", "C) By arranging in proper order the questions participants face.", "D) By guiding employees through a series of steps in team building."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How can the self-disclosure approach succeed in increasing interpersonal closeness?\n问的是原文明写的成功前提，可直接定位，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 How can the self-disclosure approach succeed in increasing interpersonal\ncloseness? 主干问该方法如何才能成功，succeed in doing 是固定搭配表示成功做到某\n事；结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】The method is well-tested and has been shown to increase interpersonal closeness. However,\nto be successful it must be voluntary.\n该方法经过充分检验，已被证明能增进人际亲密。然而，要想成功，它必须是自愿的。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nfreedom to express themselves 说让参与者自由表达，但原文规定的成功条件是\nvoluntary 自愿参加，而非表达自由，选项偷换了条件的内容。\nB) ✓ 正确\nit must be voluntary ↔ employees who volunteer to participate\n预判成功须自愿，By applying it to employees who volunteer to participate 把\nvoluntary 落实为只对自愿参与者使用，与定位句完全对应。\nC) 答非所问\narranging in proper order the questions 说按顺序排好问题，这是 guided through\na series of questions 描述的方法本身，而非题干所问的成功前提。\nD) 偷换概念\nguiding employees through a series of steps in team building 把自我表露法说成团\n建中的引导步骤，原文的 a series of questions 是问题不是团建步骤，且仍未触及自愿\n这一成功条件。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does Matous think of the various psychological methods borrowed from other fields for strengthening relationships?", "options": ["A) They must be used in combination for an entire collective.", "B) They prioritise some psychological aspects over others.", "C) They place too much stress on individual relationships.", "D) They have to be applied cautiously to be effective."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does Matous think of the various psychological methods... 虽问看法，但\nMatous 的观点在原文引语中已明说，可直接定位，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】题干 What does Matous think of the various psychological methods borrowed from\nother fields for strengthening relationships? 主干为 Matous 怎么看这些方法，think of\n表示对某事的评价，borrowed... 是后置定语修饰 methods；抓住主干即可。\n【定位】\"With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed\nfrom other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively to strengthen an entire\ncollective, which is more than just the sum of individual relationships,\" said Dr. Matous.\nMatous 博士说：\"谨慎地说，许多用于改善团队与组织的关系方法可以从其他领域借鉴。问\n题在于如何有效地运用它们来强化一个整体，而这个整体不只是个体关系的简单相加。\"\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nused in combination for an entire collective 说必须组合使用，但原文 how to apply\nthem effectively to strengthen an entire collective 说的是如何有效运用以强化整\n体，并未要求把方法组合起来。\nB) 张冠李戴\nprioritise some psychological aspects over others 说这些方法偏重某些心理层面，\n这呼应的是 numerous schools of thought that propose differing psychological\nmethods，是对各派别的描述，并非 Matous 对借用方法的看法。\nC) 与原文相反\nplace too much stress on individual relationships 说过分强调个体关系，但原文\nmore than just the sum of individual relationships 恰恰说整体不只是个体关系之\n和，提醒不要只看个体，选项把意思说反了。\nD) ✓ 正确\nWith caution ↔ applied cautiously\nhow to apply them effectively ↔ to be effective\n预判须谨慎且讲求有效，They have to be applied cautiously to be effective 同时覆\n盖 With caution 与 apply them effectively，故锁定该项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "Why did the citizens of Los Angeles vote to raise their own taxes?", "options": ["A) To combat the county's homelessness.", "B) To reform the county's service system.", "C) To fund the development of local infrastructure.", "D) To narrow the gap between the rich and the poor."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Q51 针对\"市民投票加税\"这一文中明确发生的事件追问原因，答案是文中写死的事实，不需\n要推理。\n【拆句】Q51 是一个标准疑问句，主干为 Why did the citizens vote to raise their own taxes，其\n中 to raise their own taxes 是不定式作 vote 的目的，整句结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The purpose of such action is to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of\nhousing and social services for the homeless.\n此举的目的是资助一项数十亿美元、为期十年、面向无家可归者的住房与社会服务计划。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhousing and social services for the homeless ↔ combat the county's homelessness\nThe purpose of such action ↔ To combat\n预判\"为无家可归者出钱解决问题\"与 To combat the county's homelessness 完全吻\n合，故锁定。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只说资助住房与社会服务，从未提\"改革服务体系\"，reform the county's service\nsystem 是凭 services 一词臆造出的动作。\nC) 偷换概念\n加税资助的是 housing and social services，the development of local\ninfrastructure 把\"住房与社会服务\"偷换成\"地方基础设施建设\"，二者并非一回事。\nD) 无中生有\n原文 some of the richest people in the world 只是用富裕反衬问题之刺眼，从未说\n加税是为\"缩小贫富差距\"，narrow the gap between the rich and the poor 属臆造\n目的。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do people tend to believe about the homeless?", "options": ["A) They seldom meet the descriptions of homelessness.", "B) They are the victims of fast economic development.", "C) They are responsible for their own condition.", "D) They account for the majority of drug addicts."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do people tend to believe 问的是文中明确描述过的一种社会观念，原文有现成陈\n述，属事实细节而非作者本人观点的推断。\n【拆句】Q52 主干为 What do people tend to believe about the homeless，tend to believe\n是\"倾向于相信\"的固定搭配，about the homeless 限定讨论对象，结构简单无陷阱。\n【定位】For as long as there are homeless people, there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves\nfor their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness,\nof laziness.\n只要有无家可归者存在，就有一种倾向，把他们的处境归咎于受害者自身——把他们的潦倒\n看作品格、道德缺陷或懒惰的问题。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说无家可归者\"多数并非\"精神病或瘾君子，是作者驳斥偏见，seldom meet the\ndescriptions 把\"少数符合\"歪曲成\"很少符合\"，且这是作者观点而非\"人们倾向相信\"的\n内容。\nB) 答非所问\nvictims of fast economic development 把原文 the economy forgot them（被经济\n遗忘）偷换成\"快速经济发展的受害者\"，且这是作者给出的真实成因，并非题干所问\n的\"人们倾向相信\"的偏见。\nC) ✓ 正确\nblame the victims themselves for their condition ↔ responsible for their own condition\nthe victims themselves ↔ their own\n预判\"责任在他们自己\"与 They are responsible for their own condition 直接对应，故\n锁定。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文只说\"相当一部分人\"符合吸毒等描述，account for the majority of drug addicts\n反过来说无家可归者\"占瘾君子的大多数\"，既夸大又颠倒了主从关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is one of the causes for more and more people in Los Angeles to become homeless?", "options": ["A) They find it increasingly difficult to afford a place to live.", "B) The divorce rate in the county has been on a steady rise.", "C) They have been compelled to take low-paying jobs.", "D) The society fails to forgive them for their mistakes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is one of the causes 用 one of 限定，只需在原文列举的若干成因里找出与某个选项\n对得上的那一个，是定位细节而非推断。\n【拆句】Q53 主干为 What is one of the causes，for more and more people in Los Angeles to\nbecome homeless 是介词 for 引导的长定语，修饰 causes，说明\"什么的成因\"；拆开后\n即\"越来越多洛杉矶人沦为无家可归者\"的成因，理清这层修饰即无障碍。\n【定位】Today, a greater and greater proportion of people living on the streets are there because\nof bad luck or a series of mistakes, or because the economy forgot them—they lost a\njob or were evicted or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing\nincreasingly unforgiving.\n如今，越来越多流落街头的人是因为坏运气、一连串失误，或因为被经济遗忘——他们失\n业、被逐出住所或逃离虐待性婚姻，恰逢房市变得越来越无情。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving ↔\nincreasingly difficult to afford a place to live\n预判\"房市苛刻让人难有住处\"与 They find it increasingly difficult to afford a place\nto live 吻合，故锁定。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只提个别人 fled an abusive marriage（逃离虐待婚姻），是个体经历，从未说\"全\n郡离婚率持续上升\"，divorce rate … on a steady rise 属臆造统计趋势。\nC) 无中生有\n原文说有人 lost a job（失业），并未说他们被迫去做低薪工作，compelled to take\nlow-paying jobs 与文意不符且属臆造。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 unforgiving 形容的是 housing market（房市无情），society fails to forgive\nthem for their mistakes 把\"房市\"偷换成\"社会\"、把\"无情\"曲解为\"不原谅错误\"，张冠\n李戴式偷换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do we learn from the new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report?", "options": ["A) Supplying enough job opportunities for the homeless remains a challenge.", "B) Everyone is pulling in the same direction to solve the homelessness problem.", "C) The increase in new housing falls short of the demand of the growing homeless population.", "D) Los Angeles' homeless condition is deteriorating faster than many people predict."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the new … report 问一份具名报告的内容，答案就是报告所陈述\n的事实，回原文读该报告对应句即可。\n【拆句】Q54 主干为 What do we learn from the report，new Los Angeles Homeless Services\nAuthority 是一长串前置定语修饰 report，看着唬人，实为\"洛杉矶无家可归者服务局的新\n报告\"，拆掉定语主干极简，无陷阱。\n【定位】That reality was driven home this month by a new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority\nreport showing that the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of\nnew housing.\n本月一份新的洛杉矶无家可归者服务局报告印证了这一现实：本郡无家可归人口的增长速度\n快于新住房的供给速度。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n报告比较的是\"人口\"与\"住房\"，Supplying enough job opportunities 把\"住房供给\"偷\n换成\"就业机会供给\"，报告未涉及就业。\nB) 答非所问\nEveryone is pulling in the same direction 取自报告前一句 even if everyone is\npulling in the same direction，那是作者的假设语气，并非报告内容，题干问的是\"报\n告说了什么\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing ↔\nThe increase in new housing falls short of the demand of the growing homeless population\nthe supply of new housing ↔ The increase in new housing\n预判\"住房供给跟不上需求\"与 The increase in new housing falls short of the\ndemand 完全对应，故锁定。\nD) 无中生有\n报告只说人口增长快于住房供给，从未提\"恶化速度快于许多人的预测\"，faster than\nmany people predict 凭空加入\"预测\"这一参照，属臆造。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How do most Los Angeles residents now feel about homelessness?", "options": ["A) They are increasingly ashamed about its scale.", "B) They find it no more scaring than it appears.", "C) They are less and less indifferent to it.", "D) They no longer find it shocking."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How do most Los Angeles residents now feel 看似问态度，但原文末段把居民的感受逐\n词列了出来，答案能逐字对应，按细节题回原文核对即可，无需揣摩作者立场。\n【拆句】Q55 主干为 How do residents feel about homelessness，now 是关键时间状语，强\n调\"如今\"而非过去，提醒答案要选\"当下\"的感受；句子结构简单，唯一要抓的是这个时间\n限定。\n【定位】But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed. Increasingly, we are\nuncomfortable, annoyed, disgusted, scared or unaware.\n但多数洛杉矶居民已不再震惊，也不再羞愧。我们越来越多地感到不适、恼火、厌恶、害怕\n或浑然不觉。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说居民 no longer … shamed（不再羞愧），increasingly ashamed 说\"越来越羞\n愧\"，与原文正相反。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文把 scared（害怕）列为居民如今增多的情绪之一，no more scaring than it\nappears 却说\"不比看上去更可怕\"，与原文相反。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文说居民越来越 unaware（漠然），同情 being replaced by resignation（被无奈\n取代），less and less indifferent 说\"越来越不漠然\"，与原文趋势相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmost Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed ↔\nThey no longer find it shocking\n预判\"不再震惊\"与 They no longer find it shocking 逐词吻合，故锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2023-06-1"});
